  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **recent-support gated SASRec trained with sampled cross-entropy**. Improvements remain hypotheses until measured by the pinned macro validation HR@10.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec can represent sequential intent. The user diagnosis nevertheless suggests that broad historical associations dominate some recommendations. (2) Electronics has 14.95% wrong sampled-pair rankings and a −0.4812 margin p10. These identify separation problems, although they do not measure top-10 generalization. (3) Embedding similarity scores of 0.5392–0.5566 provide little evidence of severe collapse. Prioritize intent selection and ranking supervision before diversity regularization.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) Turning Dross Into Gold motivates sampled cross-entropy as an objective experiment. Its results do not establish an optimal negative count here. (2) TLSRec motivates adaptive short/long-term fusion. History timestamps can inform that fusion without knowing the next event time. (3) R2NS motivates warming up before hard-negative mining. Unobserved positives remain a concern even after masking known interactions.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Organize interventions along three axes: evidence selection, ranking supervision, and directional relationships. SASRec supplies causal historical evidence; temporal fusion selects recent evidence; sampled cross-entropy strengthens candidate discrimination; transition residuals add directional association. None establishes equipment compatibility or confirmed ownership.\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each assessment gives a positive and a limitation.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Assessment by dimension |\n|---|---|---|\n| Uniform sampled cross-entropy [1] | 2/7/4/9/2 | O: established, reproducible; little novelty. P: scalable; benchmark dependent. U: sharper ranking; no explicit recency. M: targets separation; margins may not generalize. D: small loss change; masking needs care. |\n| Recent-support gated SASRec + sampled cross-entropy [1,2] | 4/8/8/9/4 | O: coherent combination; familiar components. P: extensible; sparse histories limit learning. U: targets stale and broad matches; cannot verify compatibility. M: targets weak margins; extra capacity may overfit. D: small branch; predictor parity required. |\n| Shrunk directed-transition residual [3] | 5/7/7/6/5 | O: asymmetric evidence; established graph concept. P: interpretable; rare edges unreliable. U: specific next-step support; association is not practical fit. M: candidate separation; popularity can dominate. D: sparse counts; causal construction adds work. |\n\n**Selected algorithm.** At every causal position, pool the last five observed embeddings and learn a scalar gate blending this representation with SASRec features. Train the fused scores using 64 uniformly sampled negatives, excluding padding, the target, and known training positives. This explicitly targets stale/isolated-history influence and broadly related recommendations lacking recent support; sampled cross-entropy addresses weak positive–negative separation.\n\nAblate objective-only and fusion-only variants. Preserve all four routes, seeds, splits, and predictor architecture parity. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10; report diagnostic margins separately. Retain the required diagnostic interfaces, and mark unavailable metadata breakdowns as unavailable.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Recent-support gated SASRec with uniform sampled cross-entropy: blend causal SASRec features with a recent-window representation, then train the fused representation to distinguish each observed next item from multiple uniformly sampled alternatives.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale or isolated historical interactions and broad similarities without current-use support. A recent-window branch gives the model an explicit way to emphasize recent specific behavior. The quantitative diagnosis identifies weak lower-tail separation, especially in Electronics; multi-negative cross-entropy directly supplies candidate competition. Neither mechanism proves compatibility, ownership, or satisfaction of a durable-device need.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four category routes, and trial-local artifacts. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS, whose supplied default is 200. For each valid position t, average the embeddings from the last min(5,t) observed nonpadding items. Compute g_t = sigmoid(MLP(concat(LayerNorm(h_t), LayerNorm(r_t)))) and z_t = LayerNorm(h_t + g_t * W_r r_t). Initialize the gate bias to -2 so training begins close to SASRec. Score items with z_t and the shared item table. Start with 64 uniform negatives per position, optionally shared across positions for efficiency; mask each position's invalid candidates and resample when necessary. Uniform sampling needs no popularity LogQ correction. Chunk logits to bound GPU memory. Do not use pretrained text encoders or unavailable ratings, categories, stores, or review text. Update both model serialization and the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the same gate and pooling operation. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Expose fused features through view.log2feats and their actual scoring operation through view.predict_score; view.model must remain the trained module. Compute the supplied margin diagnostics with their existing definitions; unavailable category breakdowns must be explicitly unavailable. No gain, simulator acceptance rate, or budget expenditure is claimed. Run the objective-only ablation first, then the gated variant, and add the fusion-only ablation if the shared budget permits.",
    "pseudocode": "for category in four_categories:\n    initialize routed SASRec and recent gate with training seed 42\n    for epoch in configured_epochs:\n        for sequence, positive in training_batches:\n            h = causal_sasrec(sequence)\n            r = causal_masked_mean(item_emb(sequence), window=5)\n            g = sigmoid(gate(concat(layernorm(h), layernorm(r))))\n            z = layernorm(h + g * recent_projection(r))\n            negatives = sample_uniform_items(K=64)\n            mask_padding_targets_and_known_training_positives(negatives)\n            logits = dot(z, item_emb(concat(positive, negatives)))\n            loss = cross_entropy(logits[valid_positions], target_column=0)\n            optimizer_step(loss)\n    save_model_config_weights_and_matching_predictor_beneath_trial()\nselect_complete_four_category_configuration_by_pinned_macro_validation_HR10()\nrun_required_diagnostics_with_fused_feature_view()",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Combines explicit recent support and candidate competition around the observed qualitative and quantitative failures.",
      "negative": "Temporal fusion and sampled cross-entropy are established techniques; the combination is an adaptation rather than a new model class."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a compact foundation for testing when recent evidence should override persistent preferences.",
      "negative": "Sparse histories and missing compatibility attributes constrain what the gate can learn, and benchmark gains are unverified."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Requires a small pooling branch, gate, and replacement loss while retaining the existing causal encoder.",
      "negative": "Negative masking, causal pooling, diagnostic adaptation, and matching predictor serialization require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
